cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "STUR Physics Lab: Gravity and the Quantum"
abstract: >-
  A science-education project and research notes, not peer reviewed. A
  plain-language guide to teleparallel gravity (TEGR), the open question of
  whether gravity is quantum, the entanglement of empty space and
  decoherence, with established physics, worked examples and open questions
  labelled separately. The research notes organise known physics
  (decoherence, Born-rule arguments, Jacobson's thermodynamic argument, TEGR)
  into one bookkeeping equation and recompute each piece in code, including
  general relativity's classic tests and a worked two-diamond design
  (phase 2.19 rad) for testing whether gravity is quantum, as perturbative
  quantum gravity predicts. Bounds on classical-quantum gravity are worked
  examples within existing literature rather than new results. A standalone
  note argues that, in 3-3-1 models, Planck-scale perturbativity selects
  three colours and three generations. Every number is reproduced by the
  scripts in src/.
authors:
